---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { dpo, links } from "../data/site";

const version = "2.2";
const updated = "18 November 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Pesa Clarity keeps on your phone, what its server holds, and your rights."
>
  <article class="container privacy-page">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="muted">
        Version {version}, last updated {updated}.
      </p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <p>
        Pesa Clarity works out your budget on your phone. Your transactions,
        messages, statements and the questions you ask about them stay on it.
        Our server does four narrow jobs and never sees a transaction.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Messages from the banks and wallets you choose. Messages from every
          other sender are ignored and never stored.
        </li>
        <li>
          On iPhone, messages come from a Shortcuts automation you set up. Each
          one waits in a file only Pesa Clarity can open until the app reads it,
          for at most 35 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          The fields read from those messages: amount, date, payee, balance and
          fee. Once a message is read, its text is deleted.
        </li>
        <li>
          Messages the app is still learning. They wait in Messages to check
          until you check them, then their text is deleted. The suggestions come
          from a reader that runs on your phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          Layouts you save with "Read messages like this the same way". A layout
          keeps the message's wording with the amount, payee, date and other
          details taken out, and numbers reduced to their shape, such as
          9,999.99.
        </li>
        <li>
          Statement PDFs. Their passwords are used once, in memory, then
          discarded. PDFs are never uploaded.
        </li>
        <li>Budgets, debts, goals, payees, categories and every insight.</li>
        <li>
          In a business space: customers' and suppliers' names and what they
          owe, the phone numbers you add or pick from your contacts (the app
          gets only the contact you pick), and the pictures and documents kept
          for receipts and invoices, such as your logo, your M-PESA QR code and
          receipts you attach.
        </li>
        <li>
          Receipts, invoices and reports are made on your phone. They leave it
          only when you share or print them, in the app or on the printer you
          choose. A Bluetooth receipt printer you pair gets only what you print.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All of this sits in an encrypted database. The key is held by your
        phone's secure hardware and released only after you unlock the app with
        your PIN, fingerprint or Face ID.
      </p>

      <h2>Ask</h2>
      <p>
        Ask answers questions about your money in your own words, such as "how
        much did I spend on food last month?". Small models that ship inside the
        app read your question and word the answer, on your phone. Every number
        comes from your own records in the space you have open.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Nothing is sent anywhere. Your questions, the answers and the records
          they come from never leave your phone, and Ask works without internet.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ask has no federated learning. "Improve the app" never includes
          anything from Ask.
        </li>
        <li>
          Conversations are kept in the space's encrypted database for as long
          as you choose in Ask settings: until you delete them, or for a year,
          90, 30 or 7 days. You can also switch saving off.
        </li>
        <li>
          Words you teach Ask, such as "chakula" for Food, stay in that space
          too.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can delete one conversation, or every conversation in a space.
          Backups leave conversations out.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What our server holds</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Sign-in</dt>
        <dd>
          Your account id, the email or Google account you sign in with, and
          when you signed up, in and out. One-time sign-in codes are deleted
          within a day of expiring, and sign-in records after 90 days. You can
          also use the app without an account; it then contacts our server only
          for the update check.
        </dd>
        <dt>Encrypted backups</dt>
        <dd>
          If you choose our vault, backups are encrypted on your phone with your
          passphrase before upload. We hold only the encrypted file and cannot
          read it. We keep your latest 10 backups and delete older ones. The
          server checks with Google (Android) or Apple (iPhone) that each backup
          comes from the genuine app; neither sees the backup. You can back up
          to your own Google Drive, your phone, an SD card or Files on iPhone
          instead.
        </dd>
        <dt>Model updates</dt>
        <dd>
          If "improve the app" is on, your phone sends clipped, noised updates
          to the categoriser, combined with other users' before we can see them.
          Never your transactions. You can switch this off at any time.
        </dd>
        <dt>Update check</dt>
        <dd>
          Once a day, with or without an account, the app asks our server
          whether a newer version or a notice is waiting, such as a fix for a
          bank that changed its messages. The request carries only the app's
          version, which build it is (Play, F-Droid, iPhone or website) and the
          platform. It carries no account, device or install id. Our server
          keeps no record of the request. The answer is signed, so the app shows
          only notices that really come from us. The app asks you to update
          before you carry on only when a version is known to get amounts wrong.
        </dd>
      </dl>

      <h2>Who runs our server</h2>
      <p>
        These services run it for us and process data only on our instructions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Google Cloud runs the server. Like any web host, it keeps standard
          request logs, such as IP address and time, for security.
        </li>
        <li>Neon stores sign-in data in its database.</li>
        <li>Cloudflare R2 stores encrypted backups.</li>
        <li>Brevo sends sign-in codes by email.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you ask us to email you a copy of this policy, Brevo sends it to the
        address you give. We use that address once and do not keep it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some of them store data outside Kenya. We use them under their data
        processing terms, as the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 requires for
        transfers abroad.
      </p>

      <h2>Exchange rates</h2>
      <p>
        To count money in other currencies, the app downloads today's exchange
        rates from a public rates service. The request carries only your base
        currency's code, such as KES; the service sees your IP address, as any
        website does. You can set your own rates instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Giving (F-Droid build)</h2>
      <p>
        The F-Droid build has a Support screen for anyone who wants to give a
        small gift by <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span>. Giving is your choice
        and never unlocks anything.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The Support screen opens CitaPay's page in your browser. Its address
          carries only the amount you chose.
        </li>
        <li>
          CitaPay, run by Citatech, and Safaricom's{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> process the payment. They see what
          any <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> payment shows, such as your
          phone number and name, plus the amount and any note you type.
        </li>
        <li>
          CitaPay's terms cover the payment:{" "}
          <a href={links.citapayTerms}>pay.citatech.cloud/legal/terms</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Pesa Clarity receives only the money and the{" "}
          <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> notice of it.
        </li>
        <li>
          The app sends nothing to CitaPay. To say thank you, it recognises your
          gift from your own <span class="nowrap">M-Pesa</span> message, on your
          phone.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <p>The app asks on first run, and you can change these any time:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Improve the app (federated updates): on by default.</li>
        <li>Contribute to licensed models: off unless you opt in.</li>
        <li>Anonymous crash reports: off unless you opt in.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Pesa Clarity has no ads, no analytics and no third-party trackers.</p>

      <h2>Keeping it safe</h2>
      <p>
        Data on your phone is encrypted, and everything sent to our server
        travels over an encrypted connection. Deleting your account removes your
        account, sign-in records and vault backups from our server.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Under the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 you can ask to see, correct or
        delete the data we hold about you, take a copy with you, and object to
        how it is used or to automated decisions. To ask, write to our Data
        Protection Officer, {dpo.name}, at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${dpo.email}`}>{dpo.email}</a>. We will answer within
        30 days. You can delete your account from inside the app. If you are
        unhappy with our answer, you can complain to the Office of the Data
        Protection Commissioner (<a href="https://www.odpc.go.ke">odpc.go.ke</a>
        ).
      </p>
      <p>Pesa Clarity is for people aged 18 and over.</p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site sets no cookies, runs no analytics and loads nothing from
        other servers. Fonts are served from this site. If you choose a light or
        dark theme, your browser keeps that choice on your device; it is never
        sent to us. The site's only script applies that choice. The web host may
        keep standard request logs, such as IP address and time, for security.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        When this policy changes, the app shows what changed and asks you to
        read it before you carry on.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Write to our Data Protection Officer, {dpo.name}, at{" "}
        <a href={`mailto:${dpo.email}`}>{dpo.email}</a>. You can also ask in
        public on GitHub (<a href={links.github}>github.com/pesaclarity</a>), or
        report a security issue privately using the security policy there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .privacy-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-7) var(--space-8);
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
